<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bulgars</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bulgar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bulgars were a Turkic-speaking nomadic people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontic-caspian-steppe">Pontic-Caspian steppe</a> who emerged in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a>. Some founded the First Bulgarian state on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> in the seventh century, while others settled on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>. Steppe peoples such as the Bulgars and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> inherited political traditions shaped by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Gokturk Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bulgars.jpg" alt="Bulgars" width="320" height="156" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Constantine Manasses · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:53-manasses-chronicle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At its top stood a mounted warrior elite of steppe origin; beneath it lived Gepids, Romanised provincials, Kutrigurs, <b>Bulgars</b> and, above all, Slavs, who formed much of the population and provided farmers and auxiliary troops.</blockquote><a href="/empire/avar-khaganate">Avar Khaganate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Bulgars</b> were a Turkic-speaking steppe people who in the early 7th century formed the confederation known as Old Great Bulgaria under Khan Kubrat, north of the Black Sea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hungarians, beaten on the Pontic steppes around 895 by <b>Bulgars</b> and Pechenegs, were moving into the Carpathian Basin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/great-moravia">Great Moravia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 469 Dengizich was defeated and killed in Thrace, and the Huns were absorbed by peoples such as the <b>Bulgars</b>; Kim argues they lived on under Ernak as the Kutrigur and Utigur Hunno-Bulgars.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hunnic-empire">Hunnic Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Turkic-speaking nomadic elites founded it and took control of the lands from the lower Volga to the northern Caucasus and the steppes north of the Black Sea, subjugating or displacing rivals such as the <b>Bulgars</b> of Kubrat's Old Great Bulgaria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Vsevolod the Big Nest defeated Gleb of Ryazan on the Koloksha in 1177, led a large expedition against the <b>Bulgars</b> in 1183, and in 1207–1208 crushed the princes of Ryazan and burned their capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vladimir-suzdal">Vladimir-Suzdal · Military</a></li></ul></section>
